        '9-12', $q$Draft: Reefs Versus Walls$q$, $q$(1) In her report "Living Edges of Calder Bay," marine ecologist Renata Okafor describes a six-year experiment that compared three ways of protecting an eroding salt marsh. (2) Salt marshes are found along many coastlines and are home to crabs, snails and wading birds. (3) Okafor's team divided a two-mile stretch of shoreline into sections protected by a concrete seawall, by restored oyster reefs, or by nothing at all. (4) After six years, the marsh behind the seawall had lost almost as much ground as the unprotected marsh, because waves bounced off the wall and scoured the mud at its base. (5) Behind the oyster reefs, by contrast, the marsh edge had actually advanced by about four feet. (6) Okafor explains that a reef will "break one wave into a hundred smaller ones" instead of throwing its full force back at the shore.

(7) ____, the reefs did more than slow erosion. (8) Okafor's divers counted three times as many young fish among the reefs as along the seawall, and the reefs grew taller each year as new oysters settled on old shells. (9) This growth matters because it means the reef barrier repairs itself, while a seawall can only weaken with age. (10) Oysters were once harvested heavily in Calder Bay and sold in markets up and down the coast. (11) Okafor does admit that young reefs need fairly calm water to take hold, and one reef planted at a site exposed to heavy storms failed in its first year.

(12) So oyster reefs are better than seawalls in every way.$q$, 'draft')
