-- Passage wg12-writing-with-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ed20a3a4-dd83-52e9-ae1c-07011d2f86b0', 'wg12-writing-with-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: Learning to Listen in "The Tuner's Apprentice"$q$, $q$(1) In Lena Okonjo-Hart's short story "The Tuner's Apprentice," a young man named Teodor spends a winter learning to tune pianos from an older craftsman, Mr. Abara. (2) At the start of the story, Teodor works quickly and checks each string against an electronic meter. (3) Okonjo-Hart writes that he "finished the church piano before the kettle boiled and left without playing a single chord." (4) Mr. Abara, by contrast, plays a slow scale after every adjustment and listens with his eyes closed. (5) Teodor is not a very good piano tuner at the beginning.

(6) Near the end of the story, Teodor returns alone to the same church piano after a cold snap has pulled it out of tune. (7) This time, the narrator says, he "left the meter in its case and played until the room seemed to agree with him." (8) The author's choice to send Teodor back to the same piano invites the reader to compare the two visits directly. (9) Pianos usually need tuning once or twice a year, depending on the weather and how often they are played. (10) ____ (11) The quiet ending suggests that Teodor has learned that his craft depends on patience and careful listening, not on speed.$q$, 'draft')
